## 5. Tailwind CSS and centralized design system

Tailwind CSS is a requirement.

The design system must be centralized so that I can adjust the brand colours, fonts and other global design decisions later without editing every component or page.

Use the supplied `tailwind_design_system_guide.docx` as the reference for the desired approach.

### Design tokens

Establish one clear source of truth for:

- Brand and accent colours.
- Page backgrounds and surface colours.
- Primary and muted text colours.
- Borders and focus colours.
- Font families and weights.
- Typography sizes and line heights.
- Common spacing and section spacing.
- Border radii and shadows.
- Any other recurring design values that are clearly visible in the screenshots.

Use semantic token names such as `brand`, `accent`, `surface`, `text-primary` and `text-muted`, adapted to the selected Tailwind version and its supported naming conventions.

The exact names are flexible; centralized control is mandatory.

### Derive the initial design system from the screenshots

Inspect the Figma PNGs and infer a practical initial set of colours, typography, spacing, radii and layout values.

Treat screenshot-derived values as estimates where exact design data is unavailable.

Do not blindly copy a colour palette from the previous ITI project if it does not match the new screenshots.

If exact Figma colour values are unavailable, select sensible approximate values that closely match the reference and keep them centralized.

### Configuration rules

- If using Tailwind CSS v4, use its supported CSS-first theme configuration where appropriate.
- If using Tailwind CSS v3, use its supported JavaScript configuration approach.
- Do not mix configuration conventions from incompatible major versions.
- Do not create redundant configuration files without a clear reason.
- Ensure that global changes to a token propagate throughout the UI.

Avoid scattering raw hexadecimal colours, repeated font-family declarations and unnecessary arbitrary values throughout the markup.

Use one-off utility values when the design genuinely requires a unique measurement, not as a substitute for a coherent design system.

Do not overengineer a design-token system or build a complex component library for this relatively small website.

