## 7. Images and icons: local assets only

Do not fetch images or icons from the internet.

Do not insert remote image URLs from stock-photo services, image search results or external websites.

### Image placeholder strategy

For this initial implementation, use a single local placeholder image in all image positions throughout the homepage.

The purpose is to let me replace the images manually after the layout is complete.

Requirements:

- Use a local image asset, not a remote URL.
- Keep the placeholder in a clearly identifiable asset location.
- Make each image's source straightforward to replace independently later.
- Preserve the appropriate dimensions, aspect ratios, crop behaviour and responsive layout shown in the design.
- Avoid broken images and dependencies on network access.
- Do not generate or source dozens of temporary images.
- If I have not supplied a placeholder image file, create or use a simple local placeholder asset where the environment supports it.

Even though the same image will initially appear in multiple positions, the implementation must preserve the intended layout and image treatment of each section.

Use sensible image loading behaviour and reserve space to reduce layout shift.

Do not lazy-load the main hero image if doing so would delay the initial page's Largest Contentful Paint. Below-the-fold images can use lazy loading when appropriate.

### Icons

I have icons in my Figma design that I will add or replace manually.

- Do not download icons from the web.
- Do not load icons from remote icon APIs.
- Do not use external image URLs for icons.
- Avoid adding a large icon library just for a small number of interface icons.
- Use simple local SVG placeholders or local asset references where appropriate.
- Keep icon references easy to locate and replace.
- Preserve icon dimensions, alignment and spacing so replacing a placeholder does not break the layout.

If an icon asset is not yet available, use a simple local placeholder rather than spending time sourcing a substitute.

